## 2. Why no tool is ever complete

Because the space of all statements is uncountable, any *listed* stock of theorems must miss something. That missing statement is true (it lives in the ceiling of all truths) but unprovable in the listed system — the abstract Goedel phenomenon. Watch it happen:

{{interactive_demo:1}}

The construction is a finite Cantor diagonal:

{{algorithm:1}}

{{demo:1}}

Since we can always adjoin the missing statement, every enumerable brain admits a strictly stronger mind tool — and ZFC is one such tool.

## 3. One theorem to settle infinitely many problems

A set-level worker settles instances one at a time; a categorical theorem settles an entire infinite family at once. Race them:

{{interactive_demo:2}}

{{visualization:2}}

{{demo:2}}

No finite amount of instance-by-instance work ever equals the single universal theorem.

## 4. The hierarchy is not a tidy ladder

One might guess all mind tools stack into a single well-ordered tower. They do not: some systems are incomparable, and there are infinite strictly *descending* chains. The Hasse diagram shows incomparability,

{{visualization:0}}

and the descending chain of tail systems shows non-well-foundedness:

{{visualization:1}}

{{algorithm:2}}

The literal well-ordering conjecture is therefore false for the natural power order — though a coarser order by proof-theoretic ordinal may yet be well-ordered.
